00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.809
so I describe her the way the workflow
sees her:

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:55.569
the woman in the purple dress, who runs
ahead of the group.

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:57.209
And to make it even more precise,

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:00.009
I add one single screenshot of her from
the video.

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.968
If your video only has one person, you do
not need this,

00:04:02.969 --> 00:04:05.129
but the moment there are more people in
the frame,

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:06.800
this screenshot makes the difference.

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:09.328
Everything else stays protected, her three
friends,

00:04:09.329 --> 00:04:11.849
the people in the background, and the
whole location.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:14.448
And a target does not have to be a person
by the way,

00:04:14.449 --> 00:04:17.448
you can replace a product in a video the
exact same way.

00:04:17.449 --> 00:04:19.688
And did you notice what is completely
missing here?

00:04:19.689 --> 00:04:21.849
Not one single word about how Lina looks,

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:24.049
because identity never comes from text,

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:25.688
it comes from the reference images,

00:04:25.689 --> 00:04:27.367
and that is exactly the next step.

00:04:27.449 --> 00:04:29.569
So this is Lina, and these three images

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:31.608
are everything the workflow gets of her.

00:04:31.609 --> 00:04:33.489
You have ten slots for references,

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:35.929
every slot has its own on and off switch,

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:37.489
and we only need three of them.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:39.369
The first image is the face anchor,

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:42.409
one clean shot of her face, straight into
the camera,

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:44.608
and this single image owns her identity.

00:04:44.609 --> 00:04:47.929
The second one is Lina complete, full body
with the outfit,

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:49.888
so the workflow knows her proportions

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:51.600
and exactly what she is wearing.

00:04:52.409 --> 00:04:54.328
And the third one is the part most people
forget:

00:04:54.329 --> 00:04:56.448
a character sheet of her from behind.

00:04:56.449 --> 00:05:00.288
In the reference video, the camera sees
her from the back half of the time,

00:05:00.289 --> 00:05:03.128
and without this sheet, Seedance would
have to guess her hair

00:05:03.129 --> 00:05:06.168
and the dress from behind, and we never
let it guess anything.

00:05:06.169 --> 00:05:09.328
Just make sure all your references show
the same character,

00:05:09.329 --> 00:05:12.489
because if they fight each other, your
result falls apart.

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:15.288
And now everything is ready for the part I
really like:

00:05:15.289 --> 00:05:17.933
Opus takes all of this, and writes the
master prompt.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.408
So Opus 5 gets the verified blueprint,

00:05:20.409 --> 00:05:23.008
